1
00:00:00,140 --> 00:00:03,840
Right now, the invisible magnetic
shield around our planet is weakening,

2
00:00:04,280 --> 00:00:08,340
and the north magnetic pole is racing
across the Arctic towards Siberia.

3
00:00:09,060 --> 00:00:11,840
One day, north and south will swap places.

4
00:00:12,540 --> 00:00:14,340
Will life on Earth survive that danger,

5
00:00:14,920 --> 00:00:17,080
or will our air blow away into space?

6
00:00:18,120 --> 00:00:18,360
No.

7
00:00:19,020 --> 00:00:20,080
We will survive.

8
00:00:20,700 --> 00:00:23,280
Life has lived through
hundreds of magnetic flips.

9
00:00:23,800 --> 00:00:27,860
So why do satellites in orbit already
have to shut down over the South Atlantic?

10
00:00:28,500 --> 00:00:31,320
How do we know Earth's
compass can turn upside down?

11
00:00:31,900 --> 00:00:35,320
And what will the night sky look
like when the poles trade places?

12
00:00:36,140 --> 00:00:37,120
1905,

13
00:00:37,580 --> 00:00:38,540
Central France.

14
00:00:39,360 --> 00:00:44,160
A physicist named Bernard Brunhes is
chipping rock from a hillside near the

15
00:00:44,200 --> 00:00:45,760
village of Pontfarein.

16
00:00:46,640 --> 00:00:50,380
He is looking at ancient lava that
poured over wet clay long ago,

17
00:00:50,840 --> 00:00:53,320
baking the mud underneath into red brick.

18
00:00:54,200 --> 00:00:59,700
As lava or brick cools, tiny grains
of iron oxide inside it lock into line

19
00:00:59,740 --> 00:01:01,280
with Earth's magnetic field,

20
00:01:01,880 --> 00:01:05,140
like microscopic compass
needles freezing in place.

21
00:01:06,340 --> 00:01:08,600
Brunhes takes samples back to his lab.

22
00:01:09,220 --> 00:01:12,560
He expects them to point north,
like the compass on his desk.

23
00:01:13,280 --> 00:01:18,440
Instead, the lava and the baked
clay both point almost exactly the

24
00:01:18,480 --> 00:01:19,240
opposite way.

25
00:01:20,140 --> 00:01:24,360
At first, scientists wonder if the
rock played a chemical trick on itself.

26
00:01:25,100 --> 00:01:27,240
Why would a hillside point the wrong way?

27
00:01:28,420 --> 00:01:31,120
23 years later, in 1929,

28
00:01:31,580 --> 00:01:36,740
Japanese geophysicist Motonori
Matuyama tests volcanic rocks across

29
00:01:36,800 --> 00:01:38,220
Japan and Manchuria.

30
00:01:39,120 --> 00:01:40,880
The younger rocks point north.

31
00:01:41,440 --> 00:01:45,300
The older ones, from early
in the Ice Age, point south.

32
00:01:46,400 --> 00:01:51,120
Today, we date that last switch
to about 780,000 years ago.

33
00:01:51,820 --> 00:01:55,360
The whole planet had turned
its magnetic field upside down.

34
00:01:56,300 --> 00:01:57,720
If the poles flipped then,

35
00:01:58,320 --> 00:02:01,980
what secret engine builds a
magnet the size of a world,

36
00:02:02,660 --> 00:02:04,420
and why does it ever lose its way?

37
00:02:05,260 --> 00:02:08,300
About 3,000 kilometres beneath your shoes

38
00:02:08,820 --> 00:02:11,760
lies the engine that runs
every compass on Earth.

39
00:02:12,700 --> 00:02:18,060
Down there, the outer core is a
churning ocean of iron-rich liquid metal

40
00:02:18,700 --> 00:02:21,120
about as hot as the surface of the Sun.

41
00:02:22,400 --> 00:02:26,060
Because Earth spins and
heat rises from the centre,

42
00:02:26,760 --> 00:02:30,920
that liquid metal twists into
giant spiralling currents.

43
00:02:32,020 --> 00:02:34,700
Moving metal carries electric currents,

44
00:02:35,180 --> 00:02:38,220
and those currents generate
our magnetic field.

45
00:02:39,320 --> 00:02:40,100
Most of the time,

46
00:02:40,640 --> 00:02:43,640
Earth's spin keeps those
iron currents lined up,

47
00:02:44,220 --> 00:02:48,000
giving us one steady north
pole and one steady south pole.

48
00:02:48,760 --> 00:02:49,180
However,

49
00:02:49,780 --> 00:02:51,560
a liquid ocean is messy.

50
00:02:52,680 --> 00:02:56,920
Swirling eddies form in the molten
iron and fight the main flow.

51
00:02:58,020 --> 00:03:01,160
What if enough backwards
eddies grow strong together?

52
00:03:01,960 --> 00:03:06,980
Then the main field weakens,
wobbles, and tips all the way over.

53
00:03:08,360 --> 00:03:10,960
Where new rock spreads
across the ocean floor,

54
00:03:11,480 --> 00:03:15,040
it records every flip like
a barcode on a packet.

55
00:03:16,060 --> 00:03:18,580
Over the last 83 million years,

56
00:03:19,100 --> 00:03:19,920
Earth has flipped

57
00:03:20,420 --> 00:03:22,380
183 times.

58
00:03:23,520 --> 00:03:28,380
And since German mathematician Carl
Friedrich Gauss began measuring the field

59
00:03:28,500 --> 00:03:30,100
strength in the 1830s,

60
00:03:30,660 --> 00:03:32,920
it has dropped by about 9%.

61
00:03:34,180 --> 00:03:35,340
Why is it dropping now?

62
00:03:35,920 --> 00:03:39,620
And where on Earth is our shield
already sagging inward today?

63
00:03:41,180 --> 00:03:43,160
New space mysteries every Sunday.

64
00:03:43,580 --> 00:03:45,440
Subscribe, and they'll come to you.

65
00:03:46,260 --> 00:03:49,580
High above South America and
the South Atlantic Ocean,

66
00:03:50,140 --> 00:03:53,100
the magnetic shield has
developed a deep dent.

67
00:03:54,240 --> 00:03:55,440
Since 2013,

68
00:03:55,940 --> 00:03:58,580
three European spacecraft called Swarm

69
00:03:59,040 --> 00:04:00,520
have mapped this weak spot,

70
00:04:00,980 --> 00:04:02,780
the South Atlantic Anomaly.

71
00:04:04,100 --> 00:04:08,260
Above most of the planet, our
field holds trapped space radiation

72
00:04:08,720 --> 00:04:11,120
safely out beyond 600 kilometres.

73
00:04:12,060 --> 00:04:17,060
Over the South Atlantic, because a patch
of magnetic field on the liquid core has

74
00:04:17,160 --> 00:04:18,640
already flipped backwards,

75
00:04:19,180 --> 00:04:22,140
the shield sags down to 200 kilometres,

76
00:04:22,720 --> 00:04:24,740
half the height of the space station.

77
00:04:26,000 --> 00:04:27,640
On the ground, you feel nothing.

78
00:04:28,260 --> 00:04:31,940
The air above you stops those
particles long before they reach you.

79
00:04:32,940 --> 00:04:36,080
Up in low Earth orbit,
it is another story.

80
00:04:37,060 --> 00:04:42,320
The Hubble Space Telescope flies through
that dent on seven to nine orbits in a row

81
00:04:42,420 --> 00:04:43,140
every day,

82
00:04:43,820 --> 00:04:48,300
and each time it stops observing
because stray radiation would swamp its

83
00:04:48,360 --> 00:04:49,020
instruments.

84
00:04:50,260 --> 00:04:54,640
Instruments on the outside of the space
station take the extra radiation too,

85
00:04:55,160 --> 00:04:59,800
and satellite operators often shut down
anything non-essential as they cross.

86
00:05:01,200 --> 00:05:02,400
Since 1970,

87
00:05:02,900 --> 00:05:06,300
that weak spot has widened
and split into two lobes.

88
00:05:07,120 --> 00:05:12,160
A weaker global field, a racing
pole, a growing dent over the

89
00:05:12,180 --> 00:05:12,880
Atlantic.

90
00:05:13,820 --> 00:05:14,700
Case closed?

91
00:05:15,520 --> 00:05:18,240
Are we overdue for a total flip right now?

92
00:05:19,140 --> 00:05:19,520
Wrong.

93
00:05:20,020 --> 00:05:24,120
We are not overdue, and today's drop
is probably just a normal breath in a

94
00:05:24,160 --> 00:05:25,180
restless planet.

95
00:05:26,340 --> 00:05:28,740
Magnetic records in
ancient rock and sediment

96
00:05:29,200 --> 00:05:34,420
show that today's field is about as strong
as it has been for 100,000 years and twice

97
00:05:34,460 --> 00:05:36,100
its average over the last million.

98
00:05:36,920 --> 00:05:40,040
Better still, the field often
dips without flipping at all.

99
00:05:40,860 --> 00:05:44,400
About 41,000 years ago,
during the Laschamp event,

100
00:05:44,840 --> 00:05:47,880
the shield tried to flip,
dropped nearly to zero,

101
00:05:48,140 --> 00:05:49,160
and snapped back.

102
00:05:50,020 --> 00:05:53,080
How did scientists unlock
that mystery year by year?

103
00:05:54,220 --> 00:05:58,020
In northern New Zealand, giant
kauri trees grew right through it,

104
00:05:58,440 --> 00:06:01,180
and their trunks were dug
out of swamp mud intact.

105
00:06:02,100 --> 00:06:05,060
As the shield dropped to about
six percent of today's strength,

106
00:06:05,600 --> 00:06:10,320
more cosmic rays hit the upper air,
creating a spike of radioactive carbon

107
00:06:10,600 --> 00:06:13,060
that every tree ring
recorded like a diary.

108
00:06:13,840 --> 00:06:18,279
The collapse took a few hundred years, and
the weak phase lasted less than a thousand

109
00:06:18,360 --> 00:06:18,720
years.

110
00:06:19,540 --> 00:06:23,420
Modern humans were already arriving
in Europe, and people from Europe to

111
00:06:23,460 --> 00:06:27,260
Southeast Asia were painting caves
and looking up at the night sky.

112
00:06:28,220 --> 00:06:33,000
Why didn't the solar wind strip away their
air and turn Earth into a second Mars?

113
00:06:33,860 --> 00:06:37,780
That is the scariest idea people
repeat about a magnetic flip.

114
00:06:38,420 --> 00:06:41,520
What if the solar wind blew
Earth's atmosphere into space?

115
00:06:42,440 --> 00:06:44,540
Let us test that fear with numbers.

116
00:06:45,520 --> 00:06:48,780
First, Earth's magnetic field
never vanishes completely.

117
00:06:49,540 --> 00:06:54,460
The main north-south magnet may weaken
to about a tenth of its normal strength,

118
00:06:55,040 --> 00:06:57,920
while smaller temporary
poles pop up around it.

119
00:06:58,860 --> 00:07:01,760
Second, we have a shield
that never switches off,

120
00:07:02,140 --> 00:07:03,240
the air itself.

121
00:07:03,960 --> 00:07:06,139
The nitrogen and oxygen above your head

122
00:07:06,600 --> 00:07:09,380
weigh about a kilogram on
every square centimetre,

123
00:07:09,880 --> 00:07:13,600
as much as a layer of water ten
metres deep covering the planet.

124
00:07:14,520 --> 00:07:17,160
Even if the magnetic field
dropped to zero tomorrow,

125
00:07:17,740 --> 00:07:22,840
almost all fast particles from space would
slam into gas molecules high in the air

126
00:07:23,240 --> 00:07:25,500
and stop long before reaching the ground.

127
00:07:26,140 --> 00:07:27,580
Why did Mars lose its air?

128
00:07:28,360 --> 00:07:32,200
Because Mars is small, with just
over a third of Earth's gravity,

129
00:07:32,700 --> 00:07:34,780
and that loss took billions of years.

130
00:07:35,380 --> 00:07:38,220
A flip on Earth takes
only a few thousand years,

131
00:07:38,840 --> 00:07:41,620
far too brief to steal a
heavy planet's atmosphere.

132
00:07:42,400 --> 00:07:44,300
Across millions of years of fossils,

133
00:07:44,760 --> 00:07:48,980
scientists find no link between
magnetic reversals and mass extinctions.

134
00:07:49,880 --> 00:07:54,220
The real job in a modern flip would be
hardening satellites and power grids

135
00:07:54,280 --> 00:07:55,500
against solar storms.

136
00:07:56,340 --> 00:07:59,960
So what would you actually see if you
stood outside in the middle of one?

137
00:08:00,800 --> 00:08:04,740
For a few thousand years, Earth
would not have two magnetic poles.

138
00:08:05,660 --> 00:08:10,319
Models of the liquid core show the field
tangling into several poles at once,

139
00:08:10,800 --> 00:08:12,860
popping up in unexpected places.

140
00:08:13,660 --> 00:08:18,440
You might have a South Pole over Africa
and a North Pole over Tahiti in the middle

141
00:08:18,480 --> 00:08:19,280
of the Pacific.

142
00:08:20,240 --> 00:08:23,860
A pocket compass would point towards
whichever pole stood nearest,

143
00:08:24,360 --> 00:08:27,320
while migrating birds,
whales, and sea turtles

144
00:08:27,820 --> 00:08:32,920
leaned on the sun, the stars, and the
coastline as their map redrew itself.

145
00:08:33,940 --> 00:08:37,580
And whenever a solar wind gust
brushed against those scattered poles,

146
00:08:38,100 --> 00:08:41,860
the aurora would not stay locked
in the Arctic and Antarctic.

147
00:08:42,620 --> 00:08:46,820
Curtains of green and crimson light would
funnel down towards the middle of the

148
00:08:46,860 --> 00:08:47,280
planet,

149
00:08:47,760 --> 00:08:52,920
shimmering above London, Cairo,
Mumbai, and Sydney on clear nights.

150
00:08:54,480 --> 00:08:57,460
For forty to four hundred
human generations,

151
00:08:57,940 --> 00:09:00,840
the northern lights would become
the lights of the whole world

152
00:09:01,460 --> 00:09:06,380
until the liquid iron deep underfoot
settled and locked the compass south.

153
00:09:07,700 --> 00:09:10,340
Our shield is not a
brittle wall that shatters.

154
00:09:10,880 --> 00:09:14,780
It is a living ocean of metal
three thousand kilometres down,

155
00:09:15,200 --> 00:09:17,120
shifting and painting the sky.

156
00:09:18,540 --> 00:09:23,600
Next, we leave Earth's shield behind
and land on Saturn's giant moon,

157
00:09:23,740 --> 00:09:24,220
Titan,

158
00:09:24,720 --> 00:09:28,240
where the air is so thick you
could strap on wings and fly.
